C++ Virtual Destructor
Description

This C++ program demonstrates the use of a virtual destructor in inheritance.

The Base class contains a virtual destructor, and the Derived class has its own destructor. A Base class pointer is used to point to a Derived object. When the object is deleted through the base pointer, both the derived and base destructors are executed correctly.

Classes Used
1. Base Class

The Base class is the base class.

It contains a virtual destructor.
The destructor displays "Base destructor".
The virtual keyword ensures proper destruction of derived objects through a base pointer.

The destructor is:

virtual ~Base()
{
    cout << "Base destructor" << endl;
}
2. Derived Class

The Derived class inherits from the Base class.

It contains its own destructor.
The destructor displays "Derived destructor".
The override keyword indicates that it overrides the virtual destructor of the base class.
Main Function

In the main() function, a Derived object is created using a base-class pointer:

Base* pointer = new Derived();

The object is then deleted using:

delete pointer;

Because the base destructor is virtual, the destructors are called in the correct order.

Output
Derived destructor
Base destructor
OOP Concept Used

Virtual Destructor

              Base
               |
       virtual destructor
               |
               ↓
            Derived
               |
       Derived destructor
               |
               ↓
          delete pointer
               |
        +------+------+
        ↓             ↓
 Derived destructor   Base destructor

A virtual destructor ensures that when a derived object is deleted through a base-class pointer, the derived destructor executes first, followed by the base destructor.